":4,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Lions are 4-2 through week 7 of the 2026 season and have outscored opponents 184 to 165. The offense has been carrying the team, ranking 4th in EPA per play while the defense sits 31st in EPA allowed. That record is better than the underlying numbers, which suggests a few close games have gone their way. What sets them apart is a play-action rate that was among the highest in the league.","offense":"The Lions have one of the most efficient offenses in the league so far, ranking in the top eight in four of the five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix has stayed close to what game situations call for. Their biggest outliers in how they line up are a play-action rate that was among the highest and the 3rd lowest shotgun rate in the league. Jared Goff has run the passing game and leaned heavily on Amon-Ra St. Brown, who has drawn 31% of the targets.","defense":"The Lions have one of the worst defenses in the league this season, ranking 25th or worse in four of the five main efficiency measures. Their one bright spot has been the pass rush, which ranks 7th in sack rate. Aidan Hutchinson has been as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise is D.J. Wonnum, who has 5 sacks while playing only 58% of the snaps, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is an eight man box rate that ranks 11th highest in the league.","special":"The Lions have been one of the most aggressive teams on fourth down, going for it on 60% of their short-yardage chances so far. Their kickers have made 100% of field goals, which is the best in the league. Their salary cap was spread across positions in a fairly typical way. Their receivers were among the oldest in the league.","players":"Jared Goff has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 1,788 yards. Amon-Ra St. Brown has been the clear top target with 31% of the looks and 525 receiving yards. Jahmyr Gibbs has handled most of the work in the backfield with 89% of the carries. On defense, Aidan Hutchinson leads the team with 5 sacks.","coaching":"Drew Petzing is calling the offense and Kelvin Sheppard is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2025 Vikings under Kevin O'Connell and the 2023 Texans under Bobby Slowik. Their defense looks most like the 2022 Cowboys under Dan Quinn and the 2024 Bengals under Lou Anarumo.","trends":"The offense went from 7th in EPA per play in 2025 to 4th so far this season, a modest improvement. The defense moved from 14th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 31st so far this season, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was screen rate, which went from 10% to 7%.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}